.TH HOMELAB-CLI 8 "2026-08-21" "homelab-cli" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
homelab-cli \- command-line client for the Homelab API
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B homelab-cli
.RB [ --api-url
.IR url ]
.I command
.RI [ subcommand ]
.RI [ args ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B homelab-cli
is a scripting client for the
.BR homelab-api (8)
REST API. It is intended for use by an account holding the
.I site_admin
role, though most subcommands work for any authenticated account
according to that account's own role permissions.
.P
A handful of session/auth commands are top-level (see COMMANDS below).
Every other REST call is grouped under a command family \- currently
.BR mail
(IMAP operations, documented in
.BR homelab-cli-mail (8)),
.BR drive
(file/directory/share operations, documented in
.BR homelab-cli-drive (8))
and
.BR admin
(site administration, documented in
.BR homelab-cli-admin (8))
\- invoked as
.RI "\(lqhomelab-cli " group " " subcommand "\(rq,"
e.g.
.BR "homelab-cli mail status" ", " "homelab-cli drive quota" ,
or
.BR "homelab-cli admin list-roles" .
.P
.B homelab-cli login
saves a session (JWT and refresh token) to a local file. Every other
command loads that session automatically and transparently refreshes
the JWT if it has expired, so a single login is normally good for the
life of the refresh token (30 days server-side), not just one hour.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI --api-url " url"
Use
.I url
as the API base URL for this invocation only. This is never written to
the cached configuration \- use
.B configure --set-api-url
to change the cached value permanently.
.TP
.B -h ", " --help
Show a list of all commands (or, given after a command/group name,
that command's or group's own subcommands/arguments). Every command
has full
.B --help
text; this man page intentionally does not duplicate per-command flag
documentation \- run
.RI "\(lqhomelab-cli " "[group] command" " --help\(rq"
for that.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS "Session & configuration"
.TP
.B login
Log in and save a session for later commands. Prompts for email/password
interactively (password via a hidden prompt \- there is no
.B --password
flag, so a credential can never leak into shell history or
.BR ps (1)
output).
.TP
.B logout
Revoke the current refresh token on the server and clear the local session.
.TP
.B configure
View or permanently change the cached API base URL.
.SS "Auth & health"
.TP
.BR validate ", " introspect ", " refresh ", " health
Check the current JWT, get the email bound to it, manually force a
refresh, or check API/database health (no auth required for
.BR health ).
.SS "Command groups"
.TP
.B mail
IMAP operations (currently: unread/total message counts). See
.BR homelab-cli-mail (8).
.TP
.B drive
File, directory, and sharing operations. See
.BR homelab-cli-drive (8).
.TP
.B admin
Role, permission, and user-management operations (requires
.I site_admin
server-side). See
.BR homelab-cli-admin (8).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/homelab-cli/config.json
Cached API base URL. Mode 0600.
.TP
.I ~/.config/homelab-cli/session.json
JWT and refresh token from the last successful
.BR login .
Mode 0600. This file contains a live bearer credential \- treat it like
an SSH private key. Deleted by
.BR logout .
.TP
.I /usr/share/bash-completion/completions/homelab-cli
Bash tab-completion for command/group names and their common flags.
Installed automatically; no user action needed as long as the
.B bash-completion
package is installed and enabled in your shell.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
None. All configuration is via the files above or the
.B --api-url
flag; there are no environment variables this tool reads.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
An API call failed (authentication, authorization, validation, or a
server error), or the local session/config could not be read as
expected.
.TP
.B 2
Argument parsing error (invalid or missing command-line arguments) \-
standard
.BR argparse (3)
behavior.
.TP
.B 130
Interrupted (Ctrl-C).
.SH SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
.I session.json
holds a live bearer credential capable of acting as the logged-in
account, including any
.I site_admin
privileges that account holds. Protect it accordingly \- it is created
mode 0600, but back up your own vigilance (e.g. don't copy your
.I ~/.config/homelab-cli
directory into a shared location or a public dotfiles repository).
See
.BR homelab-cli-admin (8)
for the additional server-side privilege requirements on
.B admin
subcommands.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR homelab-api (8),
.BR homelab-cli-mail (8),
.BR homelab-cli-drive (8),
.BR homelab-cli-admin (8)
.SH AUTHOR
Jeff Gardner <mailmasker-api01@forge.name>
